WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.866
I'm Ron Vale, and today I have the pleasure to be speaking

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:21.266
with Dr. Wally Gilbert. Dr. Gilbert started off as a

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.632
theoretical physicist, and then through an introduction

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:28.366
from Jim Watson, moved into the area of molecular

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:31.599
biology, where he's made many important discoveries

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.466
over the years. He isolated a protein that can control

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:39.932
a genetic element, the lac repressor. He's also

00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:42.466
made many important contributions in our understanding

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.199
of mRNA. But today we're going to be talking about

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.132
his seminal work on DNA sequencing. Work that

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.866
rapidly went to his being awarded a Nobel Prize

00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:58.632
in 1980. So thank you for joining us today, and

00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:03.232
maybe we can just start off by going through the history

00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:08.266
of what led you into tackle the problem of DNA sequencing.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:15.432
Okay, in the early 70s, we knew of course that the

00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:18.466
sequence of the DNA carried the ultimate code that's passed

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:23.699
from parent to offspring. But in fact, because it's essentially impossible

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.332
to actually work out the DNA sequence. I had isolated

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:31.932
the lac repressor, the protein that binds to a region of DNA.

00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:36.132
In the early 70s, I actually then tried to isolate that region

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:40.066
of DNA to which the protein bound, and we did that by

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:42.899
attaching the protein to DNA, dissolving all of the rest of

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.166
the DNA with DNase and then isolating the little fragment

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:49.732
left, protected by the protein. So I had that physical fragment in my hand.

00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:54.366
And I wanted to know its sequence. We tried developing

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:56.466
DNA sequencing methods. Nothing seemed to work.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:00.566
And we then worked out its sequence, my technician,

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:05.032
Allan Maxam and I, for about two years. By copying that little fragment

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:07.699
of DNA to RNA, and then working out the sequence of

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.999
the RNA by breaking it with enzymes and using gigantic

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.732
electrophoretic apparatuses to separate the RNA spots on

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:20.699
paper electrophoresis. It took us two years to work out

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.232
essentially 24 bases of DNA. That's about one base

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:29.399
a month. We knew that a typical gene in bacteria, we thought,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.732
the genes in our body would be in the order of 1000

00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:36.966
to 15,000 bases long. We now know of course, the genes in our body are

00:02:36.967 --> 00:02:41.632
30,000 bases long, far bigger than anyone could've imagined at the time.

00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:45.399
But at one base a month, you would never get to a thousand

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.066
bases of DNA. You would never be able to sequence that in any way.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:52.032
And that was actually the nature of the problem.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:58.766
In the early 70s. Another group worked out the sequence

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:03.999
across the entire beta-galactose genes. Again by copying

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.432
DNA and RNA. And I thought, well, that's all I interested in,

00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:12.466
the way it sequences. Go on and work on something else.

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:16.999
A Russian, Andrei Mirzabekov, came to visit me. And he wanted

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.366
to convince me to do an experiment. He wanted to convince me

00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:23.166
to look at whether the repressor bound to DNA in the large

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:26.332
groove or the small groove by modifying the DNA with chemical

00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:29.999
agents. And the agent he liked was dimethyl sulfate,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.899
which puts a methyl group on the guanine in the large groove.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.399
I thought it would be an awfully interesting experiment, but I

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.266
didn't see a way of doing it, so I didn't do anything.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:42.866
Six months later, the part of his trip to America, came through

00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:45.366
again. We had lunch, he tried again to convince me I should

00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:48.332
really do an experiment of that kind. I had the repressor,

00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:55.232
I had the DNA, I had all access to all of this. The question

00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:59.632
-- the problem was, how could I recognize where these

00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:03.499
modified bases were in the DNA, which the repressor would bind.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:08.932
In the conversations with Andrei, we thought of a way of

00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:12.966
actually trying to identify the positions of these modified bases.

00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:19.699
We had available ways of breaking the DNA with restriction enzymes,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.499
such that we could isolate a piece of DNA about a hundred

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.299
nucleotides long. Which could be labeled at one end or the other

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.466
with radioactive phosphate, depending on the way in which we

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:33.932
isolated the DNA and broken it sequentially with restriction enzymes.

00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:38.432
So we could have available a DNA molecule we knew would bind

00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:42.666
the repressor with a label at one end, and the modification

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:46.099
with dimethyl sulfate we knew would weaken the base,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.799
which the modification occurred in such a way that we could

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.832
break the DNA at that point. So we reasoned since we knew

00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:57.699
the whole sequence of this tiny little molecule, we should be able

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.066
to break the DNA at the point at which the methylations

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:05.766
occurred, and separate those molecules by size on a gel.

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:07.699
And since we knew the sequence, we should be able to

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.466
decipher which molecule came from what. So I did that

00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:14.499
experiment. I put the repressor on the DNA, modified it,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.466
second tube had no repressor on the DNA, modified it

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:24.132
with dimethyl sulfate, broke the DNA, ran gels, and the experiment

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:27.699
was a great success second time I did it. First time it didn't work

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:32.999
at all. Second time I did it, it worked perfectly. The guanine

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.532
modified the large groove, the repressor blocks the modification of

00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:39.999
guanine. The adenines were modified in the small groove,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.566
you can see yes, the repressor blocks certain adenines.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:47.232
But the striking thing about that pattern, which I had expected

00:05:47.233 --> 00:05:51.099
to have to deduce from the sequence what the pattern should

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.099
be, the striking thing about the pattern on the gel was

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.999
that it's separating the DNA fragments from the smallest

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.799
to the largest, you've got a very characteristic pattern.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.932
And you could see, oh here's DNA broken at position 30.

00:06:05.933 --> 00:06:09.732
Here's the next band, just a little higher, broken at position 31.

00:06:09.733 --> 00:06:12.932
Well, you look at that pattern and you say, this must

00:06:12.933 --> 00:06:16.132
be the sequencing method. It's sufficiently clear that

00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:19.199
in one go I could read essentially a hundred bases of DNA.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.232
So was there a eureka moment?

00:06:21.233 --> 00:06:22.832
Well, there's a moment when you look at that and you say,

00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:25.366
clearly this is a sequencing method. The only problem is

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:30.099
can you actually sequence just by looking at the A's and

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.132
G's? We actually sequenced something which we never

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:34.466
published just by looking at the A's and G's coming down

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:38.099
the two strands. And we started looking for chemical reagents that

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.499
would break the DNA at cytosines and thymines. Now, in fact,

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.966
we knew of such reagents immediately, because I knew hydrazine

00:06:45.967 --> 00:06:50.999
broke DNA at cytosines and thymines. That's part of the

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.266
old chemistry that we tried five years earlier, trying to work out

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:58.299
a sequencing method. So we could immediately get a display

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.866
of the C's and the T's. Allan Maxam then spent about 9 months

00:07:02.867 --> 00:07:06.366
trying to find a way of changing that display so you could

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:09.132
distinguish the C's and the T's. Essentially by throwing

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:12.132
every chemical in the laboratory into the reaction to see

00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:15.432
if it made a difference. And he came up with a modification

00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:21.199
by adding salt to the reaction. And we ended up with effectively

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.866
four reactions, each one of which was specific enough

00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:29.732
to reveal a breakage of DNA at a certain base. And

00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:32.932
the trick behind the sequencing was this idea of measuring

00:07:32.933 --> 00:07:37.432
a distance from the wavelength of the labeled phosphate

00:07:37.433 --> 00:07:42.132
to the position of each base. The first gels would display

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:47.466
molecules on the order from 10 to 50-60 long, you could read that

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:51.066
sequence. We very quickly went to gels that could display molecules

00:07:51.067 --> 00:07:55.366
hundreds long. And you could read in an afternoon

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:58.432
more than one base a month, and in an afternoon

00:07:58.433 --> 00:08:02.532
you could read several hundred bases. The next

00:08:02.533 --> 00:08:07.832
I had had a previous student, Nancy Maizels, who had worked out

00:08:07.833 --> 00:08:11.399
63 bases of DNA by copying an RNA for her thesis.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.566
The next thesis was about 1500 bases of DNA, was

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:20.966
the sequence of the repressor gene itself worked out from the

00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:24.432
DNA. In that case, correcting the protein sequence, which

00:08:24.433 --> 00:08:27.799
there were mistakes. And that was the next student's thesis.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:32.132
The student after that did 5000 bases of DNA that did an entire

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:38.632
plasmid. We stopped giving PhDs for sequencing. The method was actually

00:08:38.633 --> 00:08:43.899
developed around 1976, '75 or '76, and we gave the method

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:47.466
away at Gordon Conferences. The Gordon Conference that

00:08:47.467 --> 00:08:50.966
summer. We gave a little handout and people began to sequence

00:08:50.967 --> 00:08:55.366
DNA immediately. The paper, the official paper published on it

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:59.566
was probably not published until 1976, or 1978. By that time,

00:08:59.567 --> 00:09:02.232
people came to the laboratory to learn the sequencing

00:09:02.233 --> 00:09:03.232
methods and took it away.

00:09:03.233 --> 00:09:08.199
How long did it take from the time when you were working

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.666
on the problem from the repressor point of view to developing

00:09:11.667 --> 00:09:13.466
this general method?

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:16.799
The total time was 9 months, to find the last bit of the

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.399
chemistry. And the sequencing came -- the fact that you could

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.799
do the sequencing came immediately out of that first experiment.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.799
That immediately said, oh it's clear that the patterns are distinct

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.699
enough. Fred Sanger was working on the problem

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.199
simultaneously, trying to develop sequencing methods based

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.432
again on ultimately the same idea, can I measure a distance

00:09:41.433 --> 00:09:45.266
along the DNA to a position at which a base occurs?

00:09:45.267 --> 00:09:50.566
The gels that he used came out of my laboratory, gels that he

00:09:50.567 --> 00:09:54.166
also used came out of the technology that we had developed

00:09:54.167 --> 00:09:59.032
in terms of sizing DNA molecules. And most of that technology

00:09:59.033 --> 00:10:01.866
had been developed earlier just to size DNA molecules

00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:05.066
for the whole problem of finally isolating the DNA fragment

00:10:05.067 --> 00:10:06.132
that was bound to the repressor.

00:10:06.133 --> 00:10:11.299
And what was the reception from the community when

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.032
you first showed these gels at meetings?

00:10:15.033 --> 00:10:18.366
People began to sequence immediately. I mean the sequencing

00:10:18.367 --> 00:10:21.766
took off at that point, both Fred's method became available

00:10:21.767 --> 00:10:24.566
and our method at essentially the same time in 1976.

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:29.132
And people around the world began to sequence actively

00:10:29.133 --> 00:10:34.399
DNA. The rate of DNA sequencing started to go up following

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.566
Moore's Law, doubling every 18 months. So from that time

00:10:38.567 --> 00:10:43.832
until probably 2010, it may be going even faster now.

00:10:43.833 --> 00:10:50.099
So just looking at how the field has progressed since then,

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:57.366
when you first developed this method, did you have any

00:10:57.367 --> 00:11:03.132
idea or anticipation of how the field of DNA sequencing

00:11:03.133 --> 00:11:04.999
might develop?

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:11.866
It's a hard question. In the middle '70s, when we developed the method,

00:11:11.867 --> 00:11:15.366
and by the late '70s, it was clear you could decipher

00:11:15.367 --> 00:11:21.666
any gene. And that's what people were doing all over, everywhere.

00:11:21.667 --> 00:11:26.266
And the Nobel Prize is actually for the recombinant technology

00:11:26.267 --> 00:11:30.566
It was given half to Paul Berg for standing in for all the

00:11:30.567 --> 00:11:32.666
people who showed you could move the DNA fragment

00:11:32.667 --> 00:11:36.299
around from one cell to another, put it in a plasmid,

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.832
grow a human DNA fragment in a bacterial cell.

00:11:40.833 --> 00:11:44.832
And to Fred and myself for the ability to decipher the DNA.

00:11:44.833 --> 00:11:47.299
It wouldn't do much good if you could move DNA around

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:51.332
if you couldn't decipher it. Across that period people were

00:11:51.333 --> 00:11:59.166
then sequencing genes like mad. Around 1980, it was essentially

00:11:59.167 --> 00:12:02.632
the workhorse, the sequencing methods of those times. Fred's

00:12:02.633 --> 00:12:06.732
and mine were being used equally. And continued to be

00:12:06.733 --> 00:12:12.366
equally until the middle '80s, when the machines came in and

00:12:12.367 --> 00:12:16.232
which the dideoxy method was mostly adapted to.

00:12:16.233 --> 00:12:20.666
And once the machines came in, Fred's method took over.

00:12:20.667 --> 00:12:24.666
Almost entirely, the chemical method is used only for very special

00:12:24.667 --> 00:12:27.866
cases. It could do certain things that the dideoxy method

00:12:27.867 --> 00:12:31.999
can't do. It can reveal the methylation pattern and things like that.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.966
But beyond that, it became sort of for specialty use.

00:12:36.967 --> 00:12:41.032
I did not anticipate at the time we discovered the DNA

00:12:41.033 --> 00:12:46.266
sequencing, the genome. In fact in 1985, I was invited

00:12:46.267 --> 00:12:53.899
to a meeting in California by Bob Sinsheimer, and

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:59.466
with a sort of idea, could one sequence the human genome?

00:12:59.467 --> 00:13:01.699
And I went to that meeting thinking, that's the silliest

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.132
thing I ever heard of. That's so much larger than things we're

00:13:05.133 --> 00:13:09.432
sequencing up till now. And at the meeting, I thought about

00:13:09.433 --> 00:13:14.432
where the technology has gotten to. I thought finally,

00:13:14.433 --> 00:13:17.132
it's probably possible to sequence the genome. I became,

00:13:17.133 --> 00:13:20.799
at that time in 1985, an advocate of the idea that yes, you

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:24.132
should sequence the genome. Originally, I thought it would be

00:13:24.133 --> 00:13:28.499
a 30 year problem, as I suggested to Bob. And he said

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:33.099
to sequence the genome, 10 years for the first 1%,

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:37.366
10 years for the next 10%, and 10 years for the last. And do it

00:13:37.367 --> 00:13:40.866
on an industrial scale, but a 30 year problem.

00:13:40.867 --> 00:13:45.932
It actually took about 15 years, it was a purely industrial

00:13:45.933 --> 00:13:51.132
scale problem. And I realized in '85, the technology that

00:13:51.133 --> 00:13:55.299
then existed was improving at a rate such that you could do

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:58.132
the entire human genome in a reasonable amount of time.

00:13:58.133 --> 00:14:02.066
Of course by now, the technology has changed radically from

00:14:02.067 --> 00:14:07.299
the first ones we had. We're now at least at third or fourth

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.799
generation of sequencing, the machines and technologies

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:16.566
out there. And whereas, originally it was one base a month,

00:14:16.567 --> 00:14:19.432
it's now billions and billions of bases in an afternoon.

00:14:19.433 --> 00:14:25.899
I find it sort of unimaginable to have been part of this entire

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:30.299
effort, but in fact, to have seen it go from nothing. At the time,

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:34.466
we were doing this first DNA sequence, Jim would be

00:14:34.467 --> 00:14:37.632
only too willing to assure me that people would never be able,

00:14:37.633 --> 00:14:41.332
off in a decade, be able to sequence genes.

00:14:41.333 --> 00:14:48.132
It gives an amazing example of how fast science is moving

00:14:48.133 --> 00:14:48.732
these days.

00:14:48.733 --> 00:14:49.699
That's right.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.867
Especially in the biological sciences.

